# CI/CD Pipeline Reference

## Java Maven Build & Versioning Strategy

> **Version Pattern:** `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` (e.g., `1.1.0`)  
> **Branch Model:** GitHub Flow

-----

## 1. Overview

This document describes the CI/CD pipeline design for Java Maven-based applications built on a GitHub Flow branching strategy. It covers automatic and manual build triggers, version management, artifact promotion, and environment deployment rules across two primary branch types: **master** and **release**.

> **Key Rule:** `SNAPSHOT` suffixes denote development builds. Only release artifacts (without `SNAPSHOT`) are eligible for staging and production environments.

-----

## 2. Versioning Conventions

|Identifier      |Example         |Description                                 |
|----------------|----------------|--------------------------------------------|
|SNAPSHOT build  |`1.1.0-SNAPSHOT`|Development/CI artifact — DEV and UAT only  |
|Release artifact|`1.1.0`         |Stable, promotable — STG through PRD        |
|MINOR bump      |`1.1 → 1.2`     |Triggered on master after release branch cut|
|PATCH bump      |`1.1.1 → 1.1.2` |Triggered on release branch after promotion |

-----

## 3. Environment Deployment Rules

|Artifact Type    |DEV / UAT|STG|PRE-PRD / PRD-BETA|PRD|
|-----------------|:-------:|:-:|:----------------:|:-:|
|`1.1.0-SNAPSHOT` |✅        |❌  |❌                 |❌  |
|`1.1.0` (release)|✅        |✅  |✅                 |✅  |

-----

## 4. Master Branch Pipeline

Active pom.xml version: `1.1.0-SNAPSHOT`

The master branch is the primary integration branch. Its `pom.xml` always carries a `SNAPSHOT` version, reflecting ongoing development. Two execution modes are supported.

-----

### 4.1 Standard Build — Auto-Trigger & Manual (no parameters)

|Trigger Type      |Condition          |Outcome                            |
|------------------|-------------------|-----------------------------------|
|Auto-trigger      |PR merged to master|Initiates CI build immediately     |
|Manual (no params)|Human-initiated    |Behaves identically to auto-trigger|

**Steps:**

1. Build the Maven package at version `1.1.0-SNAPSHOT`.
1. Publish the artifact to Artifactory: `local-dev`.
1. Deploy to **DEV** and **UAT** only. Deployment to STG, PRE-PRD, PRD-BETA, and PRD is not permitted for SNAPSHOT artifacts.

-----

### 4.2 Promote to Release — Manual (`PROMOTE_DEV=true`)

|Trigger Type             |Condition                 |Outcome                                   |
|-------------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------------------|
|Manual (PROMOTE_DEV=true)|Human-initiated with param|Executes full release branch creation flow|

**Steps:**

1. Remove the `SNAPSHOT` suffix from `pom.xml` — version becomes `1.1.0`.
1. Tag the master branch commit with the release version: `1.1.0`.
1. Build the Maven package at version `1.1.0`.
1. Publish the release artifact to Artifactory: `local-release`.
1. Create release branch: `release/1.1`.
1. On `release/1.1`: bump `pom.xml` to `1.1.1-SNAPSHOT` (PATCH increment).
1. Push the release branch changes to the remote repository.
1. Checkout master branch.
1. On master: bump `pom.xml` to `1.2.0-SNAPSHOT` (MINOR increment).
1. Push the master branch changes to the remote repository.

-----

## 5. Release Branch Pipeline

Initial pom.xml version on `release/1.1`: `1.1.1-SNAPSHOT`

Release branches (e.g., `release/1.1`) are created from master during the `PROMOTE_DEV` flow. They carry their own PATCH-incrementing SNAPSHOT version and support the same two execution modes as the master pipeline.

-----

### 5.1 Standard Build — Auto-Trigger & Manual (no parameters)

|Trigger Type      |Condition                  |Outcome                            |
|------------------|---------------------------|-----------------------------------|
|Auto-trigger      |PR merged to release branch|Initiates CI build immediately     |
|Manual (no params)|Human-initiated            |Behaves identically to auto-trigger|

**Steps:**

1. Build the Maven package at version `1.1.1-SNAPSHOT`.
1. Publish the artifact to Artifactory: `local-dev`.
1. Deploy to **DEV** and **UAT** only. Deployment to STG, PRE-PRD, PRD-BETA, and PRD is not permitted.

-----

### 5.2 Promote Release — Manual (`PROMOTE_RELEASE=true`)

|Trigger Type                 |Condition                 |Outcome                             |
|-----------------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------------|
|Manual (PROMOTE_RELEASE=true)|Human-initiated with param|Executes full release promotion flow|

**Steps:**

1. Remove the `SNAPSHOT` suffix from `pom.xml` — version becomes `1.1.1`.
1. Tag the release branch commit with version: `1.1.1`.
1. Build the Maven package at version `1.1.1`.
1. Publish the release artifact to Artifactory: `local-release`.
1. Push changes to the remote repository.
1. Checkout the `release/1.1` branch.
1. Bump `pom.xml` to `1.1.2-SNAPSHOT` (PATCH increment) on the release branch.
1. Push the release branch changes to the remote repository.

-----

## 6. Upper-Environment Deployment Sequence

Once a release artifact (e.g., `1.1.1`) has been published to `local-release`, it follows a sequential promotion path. Each gate requires validation before proceeding.

|#  |Environment |Notes                                                                        |
|:-:|------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|1  |**STG**     |First upper-environment gate. Regression and integration testing occurs here.|
|2  |**PRD-BETA**|Beta release validation with limited production traffic.                     |
|3  |**PRE-PRD** |Final pre-production validation before full production deployment.           |
|4  |**PRD**     |Full production deployment. Triggers post-deployment cherry-pick to master.  |


> **Note:** Following a successful PRD deployment, a cherry-pick from the release branch must be executed to merge the production changes back into master, ensuring branch parity.

-----

## 7. Post-Production Merge (Cherry-Pick to Master)

After a successful production deployment, the release-specific changes must be reflected in master. This is achieved through a **cherry-pick** from the release branch — not a direct merge — to preserve a clean and auditable commit history on master.

-----

## 8. Release Branch Lifecycle & Retention

|Attribute       |Detail                                                                                        |
|----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Retention period|2 months from initial PRD deployment, or until the next major release — whichever comes first.|
|Deletion trigger|Expiry of the retention window or superseded by next release branch.                          |
|Patch source    |All STG/PRD regression fixes branch from the release branch, not master.                      |

-----

## 9. Regression & Production Defect Resolution

Defects identified in upper environments are resolved via short-lived feature branches created from the release branch. Fixes flow through the standard CI pipeline and validation gates before re-promotion.

### 9.1 Regression Fix (Defect Found in STG)

1. Create a feature branch from `release/1.1` targeting the specific regression.
1. Implement the fix and raise a Pull Request against the release branch.
1. Upon PR merge into `release/1.1`, the standard CI pipeline triggers automatically (producing e.g., `1.1.2-SNAPSHOT`).
1. Execute `PROMOTE_RELEASE` to publish `1.1.2` and advance the release branch to `1.1.3-SNAPSHOT`.
1. Deploy `1.1.2` through the upper-environment sequence: STG → PRD-BETA → PRE-PRD → PRD.

### 9.2 Production Defect Fix (Defect Found in PRD)

1. Create a feature branch from the release branch corresponding to the affected production version.
1. Implement the fix and raise a Pull Request against the release branch.
1. Upon PR merge, the standard CI pipeline triggers automatically.
1. Execute `PROMOTE_RELEASE` to publish the next patch release.
1. Deploy through the upper-environment sequence: STG → PRD-BETA → PRE-PRD → PRD.
1. After successful PRD deployment, cherry-pick changes back to master.

-----

## 10. Full Version Progression Example

End-to-end version lifecycle from initial SNAPSHOT through multiple patch releases and defect fixes.

|Event                    |Branch                |Artifact        |Next SNAPSHOT            |
|-------------------------|----------------------|----------------|-------------------------|
|PR merged to master      |`master`              |`1.1.0-SNAPSHOT`|—                        |
|PROMOTE_DEV              |`master → release/1.1`|`1.1.0` ✅       |master: `1.2.0-SNAPSHOT` |
|PR merged to release/1.1 |`release/1.1`         |`1.1.1-SNAPSHOT`|—                        |
|PROMOTE_RELEASE          |`release/1.1`         |`1.1.1` ✅       |release: `1.1.2-SNAPSHOT`|
|STG regression fix merged|`release/1.1`         |`1.1.2-SNAPSHOT`|—                        |
|PROMOTE_RELEASE          |`release/1.1`         |`1.1.2` ✅       |release: `1.1.3-SNAPSHOT`|
|PRD defect fix merged    |`release/1.1`         |`1.1.3-SNAPSHOT`|—                        |
|PROMOTE_RELEASE          |`release/1.1`         |`1.1.3` ✅       |release: `1.1.4-SNAPSHOT`|
